// Data of the theorem graph (theorems.html): theorems, lemmas, corollaries and conjectures of combinatorics, MSC 2020
// 05, with the border classes 11B, 11P, 52B and 52C, which have no folder of their own yet (rule 1 of ../../CLAUDE.md).
// In English. Built on 3 November 2026 from data/theorems.js (older model of the maps, which stays unchanged for
// ../../../maps/entwurf-12/), with new ids. The collective nodes of that file (foundations of graph theory, spectral graph
// theory, random graphs, elementary counting, matroids) are split into single lemmas where a proof uses them; results of
// linear algebra, topology, probability and harmonic analysis have the area "basic" and `foundation: true`. The
// Chevalley–Shephard–Todd theorem (first class 13A) moved to ../../commutative-algebra/ (ca:chevalley-shephard-todd).
//
// Model as in ../../algebraic-number-theory/data/theorem-graph.js (see its header comment): `premises` and `proof` are
// the standard textbook proof, named in `proofName`; further proofs in `alternativeProofs`; `foundation: true` marks
// general tools and results resting on theory outside these graphs. `area` is the three-character class, as in
// data/concepts.js. `concepts` are ids of data/concepts.js or carry the prefix of another folder.
// References to theorems of other folders: "st:", "ent:", "gt:" (Burnside's lemma, sign, classification of finite
// simple groups), "ca:" (division of polynomials, Cohen–Macaulay rings), "ag:", "ar:" (modular forms, finite fields,
// Hasse–Minkowski), "ant:", "rt:" (property (T), Weyl–Kac formula, characters of the symmetric group).
//
// Graph theory (05C) is an area of its own since 4 November 2026: its theorems moved to ../../graph-theory/ ("gr:").
// Prefix for references from other folders: "co:<id>". Anchors: ramsey-theorem, szemeredi-theorem, rsk-correspondence.
// Sources: as in data/theorems.js (original papers and textbooks per proof), in particular Diestel, Graph Theory (5th ed.
// 2017); Aigner and Ziegler, Proofs from THE BOOK (6th ed. 2018); Alon and Spencer, The Probabilistic Method (4th ed.
// 2016); Stanley, Enumerative Combinatorics; Andrews, The Theory of Partitions (1976). Not checked by a specialist.
// Checked by ../../tests/check-theorems.js.
// `fromDefinitions: true` marks a theorem whose usual proof uses only definitions and axioms of its area and no
// theorem (it has no premises); a theorem without premises that rests on theory outside these graphs carries
// `foundation: true`, and its `proof` names that theory. tests/check-theorems.js warns about theorems without
// premises that have neither mark.

